# Goblin — first port from the WAM cast (~/Projects/2026/horse/models/goblin.wam),
# and the first character authored in .blob that was not the zombie.
#
# WAM builds bodies from `loft`s: a profile swept through rings along a bone
# chain. .blob has no loft, so every ring becomes a `blob` at the same relative
# position. That mapping is closer than it sounds — a ring IS an ellipse on a
# bone, which is what a blob is — but two things do not survive it:
#
#   * WAM's `w`/`d` are FULL width and depth; a blob's `r` is a radius. So
#     r = w/2, and deep = d/w to keep the cross-section's aspect.
#   * WAM rings run over a bone CHAIN with t normalised across the whole chain;
#     .blob prims ride ONE bone. Every `at` below was recomputed by walking the
#     chain length. The torso chain is pelvis(0.14) + spine1(0.10) + chest(0.12)
#     = 0.36, so WAM's t=0.58 lands 0.209 along, i.e. spine1 at 0.69.
#
# Silhouette intent, carried over from the WAM file: stooped and scheming, not
# standing at attention. Spine pitches forward, neck pitches back to bring the
# face level, arms hang long and slightly ahead, legs bow out at the thigh and
# back in at the shin. None of that is animation — it is all rest pose.
model goblin
  height 1.15

skeleton
  root pelvis at 0.425

  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=13 len=0.10
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=9  len=0.12
  # Neck pitches BACK against the spine's forward lean, or the goblin stares at
  # its own feet. -17 then -5 lands the face roughly level.
  #
  # WAM's neck is 0.045 and that works there, because its neckcol is a separate
  # mesh sitting proud of the torso. Here the chest's top blob (r 0.143) simply
  # swallowed a 0.045 neck whole and the head sat straight on the shoulders.
  # Lengthened until the head clears the shoulder mass.
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-17 len=0.140
  # Named `skull`, not `head` as in the WAM file: facePrims() hardcodes that
  # bone name, and a .blob character without one throws from resolveBones
  # before validation ever runs.
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-5 len=0.235

  mirror
    # WAM's own comment says the shoulder must land INSIDE the chest mass or
    # "the arm floats free". That is right for WAM, where the arm is a separate
    # mesh that has to overlap to look attached — and exactly WRONG here. In an
    # SDF, overlapping IS merging: at len=0.058 the shoulder sat at x 0.13
    # against a chest half-width of 0.146, and smooth-min swallowed both arms
    # into the torso completely. But overshooting is just as wrong: at len=0.115
    # fusedOf(torso, armL) went POSITIVE (+0.0027), i.e. the core-to-core path
    # left the flesh and the arm was hanging on by nothing. 0.092 is the band
    # between swallowed and detached — the checks found both edges of it.
    # (WAM also tilts this -14; dropped, because on a `side` base tilt swings
    # toward +y, not +x, so it does not mean what it means on the arms.)
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.080 dir=side   len=0.092
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=-14 tilt=9 len=0.17
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=13     len=0.17
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down              len=0.055
    # Bowed legs: splay out at the thigh, back in at the shin, so the knee
    # stands proud of the ankle line.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis   dir=down tilt=8  pitch=-9 side=0.092 len=0.195
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down tilt=-8 pitch=11 len=0.19
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd               len=0.10
  end

body
  # Pot belly. The widest ring sits low and forward — a goblin's gut hangs over
  # the belt. .blob cannot split the depth axis the way WAM's dtop/dbot do, so
  # the gut is symmetric front-to-back here; the back reads flatter in WAM.
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.00 r=0.128 deep=0.86 blend=0.0075
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.87 r=0.173 deep=0.84 blend=0.0080
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.69 r=0.165 deep=0.82 blend=0.0080
  blob torso on chest  at=0.46 r=0.146 deep=0.82 blend=0.0075
  blob torso on chest  at=1.00 r=0.108 deep=0.82 blend=0.0060

  # Neck column.
  #
  # No-neck syndrome took FOUR changes, and the non-obvious one is the head.
  #
  # facePrims centres the cranium partway along the skull bone, so with
  # headRadius 0.150 on a 0.18 skull bone the bottom of that ellipsoid sat
  # BELOW the neck's top — the head was not merely close to the shoulders, it
  # enclosed the entire neck, and no amount of thinning the column could show
  # something buried inside a sphere. Shrinking the cranium (0.150 -> 0.126)
  # and lengthening the skull bone (0.18 -> 0.235) lifts it clear.
  #
  # The other three: a longer neck bone (0.085 -> 0.140), a thinner column, and
  # a smaller chest cap for it to rise out of. None of them works alone.
  bar head on neck from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.048 deep=0.94 blend=0.0016

  # Ears: small and pointed, not round flaps.
  #
  # A single fat ellipsoid gives a Mickey Mouse ear — an ellipsoid cannot taper,
  # so it is round whatever you scale it to. Two prims per ear can: a flattened
  # base, then a much smaller tip pushed further out and up. The taper between
  # them under smooth-min is what reads as a point.
  #
  # `both` mirrors the offset across a bone that is not itself mirrored — the
  # same mechanism the zombie's eye sockets use.
  blob head on skull at=0.60 r=0.040 wide=0.75 tall=1.45 deep=0.40 blend=0.0022 offset=(0.098,0.028,-0.010) both
  blob head on skull at=0.66 r=0.020 wide=0.55 tall=1.30 deep=0.34 blend=0.0018 offset=(0.128,0.082,-0.020) both

  # Nose — shaded by the sheet for the front view, built for the PROFILE.
  #
  # The sheet's noseRidge is what makes a nose read head-on, and it does that
  # job well. It does nothing for the silhouette, though, and the first version
  # here did not either: at offset z 0.062 with a half-depth of 0.046 the tip
  # reached z 0.108, against a skull semi-depth of 0.126 x 0.94 = 0.118. The
  # nose was entirely INSIDE the cranium — a slight bump in profile because
  # that is all it geometrically was.
  #
  # Pushed out past the skull surface, and split in two so it hooks: the tip
  # sits lower and further forward than the bridge. That is WAM's bent `sweep`
  # approximated with the two prims .blob has.
  #
  # This is the tradeoff face.ts's header names: a nose that juts past the
  # planar face projection picks up texture from behind it and reads flatter
  # than the shading suggests. Accepted deliberately here — the owner wants the
  # profile — but it is why the ridge shading carries the front view rather
  # than the geometry being pushed further still.
  blob head on skull at=0.46 r=0.029 wide=0.85 deep=2.05 blend=0.0032 offset=(0,-0.020,0.094)
  blob head on skull at=0.42 r=0.019 wide=0.68 deep=1.70 blend=0.0026 offset=(0,-0.058,0.118)

  # Spindly arms with a knobbly elbow.
  #
  # These were five blobs, one per WAM ring, and they read as a STRING OF BEADS.
  # A loft is a continuous swept surface, so ring-per-slice is natural in WAM; in
  # an SDF the equivalent of a tapering loft is a CAPSULE, and blobs are for the
  # lumps that interrupt it. Shafts are bars now, and the one surviving blob is
  # the elbow knob — the thing the taper exists to sell.
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.050 blend=0.0045 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=0.00        r=0.049 blend=0.0050 mirror
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.037 blend=0.0045 mirror

  # Big grabby hands. Goblins are all nose, ears and hands.
  blob arm on hand at=0.45 r=0.056 deep=0.91 blend=0.0050 mirror

  # Bowed legs, same treatment: shaft as a bar, calf bulge as the one blob.
  # The bulge sits at the knee joint, not mid-shin.
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.02 to=0.95 r=0.068 blend=0.0050 mirror
  blob leg on shin  at=0.11         r=0.064 blend=0.0050 mirror
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.048 blend=0.0045 mirror

  # Splayed, oversized feet — they anchor a top-heavy silhouette.
  blob leg on foot at=0.50 r=0.070 tall=0.75 deep=0.90 blend=0.0045 mirror

# The head itself is facePrims(), not geometry authored here — .blob gets the
# zombie's parameterised skull whatever character it is. Pushed toward goblin
# proportions: a bigger cranium relative to a 1.15 m body, a jutting jaw for the
# snout, and a heavier brow for the scowl. The pointed cranium taper and the
# tusks in the WAM file have no analog at all.
face
  headRadius 0.126
  headWidth  0.96
  headHeight 1.02
  headDepth  0.94
  jawWidth   0.70
  jawHeight  0.82
  jawDrop    0.072
  jawJut     0.072
  noseLength 0.006
  noseWidth  0.34
  noseDrop   0.012
  browHeavy  0.014
  browRise   0.038
  headBlend  0.0030

# The goblin's own face, generated rather than inherited.
#
# Before this, every .blob character wore /assets/lab/zombie-face.png, because
# a shared 64x64 greyscale sheet was the only way to have a face at all — which
# is most of why this thing still read as "the zombie with different limbs".
# The sheet is drawn from these numbers instead: features are DARKENINGS of a
# grainy base, because the shader uses the sheet as an albedo multiplier.
#
# Small close-set eyes under a heavy slanted brow, and a wide down-turned mouth
# — a scowl is mostly brow angle and mouth curve, not eye shape.
sheet
  eyeGap     0.30
  eyeSize    0.072
  eyeRise    0.44
  eyeTilt    16
  browHeavy  0.90
  browAngle  24
  mouthWidth 0.54
  mouthRise  0.73
  mouthCurve -0.50
  mouthOpen  0.075
  nostril    0.45
  noseRidge  1.00
  noseWide   0.080
  noseLong   0.235
  jawShade   0.55
  grain      0.10
  seed       5
